{:edb/db-before-t 2380 :edb/db-after-t 2381 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535693 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:19:05.359Z" 13194139535693 true] [17592186175892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361620 13194139535693 true] [17592186175992 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361627 13194139535693 true] [17592186175992 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361634 13194139535693 true] [17592186175993 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361641 13194139535693 true] [17592186176107 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535693 true] [17592186176107 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation $ϕ = \\frac{π}{4}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" 13194139535693 false] [17592186176107 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation $ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" 13194139535693 true] [17592186176107 1145 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis must equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-cone.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/27f9b580436d8e1aebeb4f150643a22266e9ad7f488b98e63f8a49dd4f1aa97f.png)" 13194139535693 true] [17592186361620 1017 #edb/ref 17592186361621 13194139535693 true] [17592186361620 1089 "q-135302" 13194139535693 true] [17592186361620 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535693 true] [17592186361620 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" 13194139535693 true] [17592186361620 1113 #uuid "91c21edd-cba7-59db-be76-718b72384d4c" 13194139535693 true] [17592186361620 1145 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/0043ffdc885e7edeebb7c4df571466fe4f13dfb08728290cd6d54f47777a7179.png)" 13194139535693 true] [17592186361621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361622 13194139535693 true] [17592186361621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361623 13194139535693 true] [17592186361621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361624 13194139535693 true] [17592186361621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361625 13194139535693 true] [17592186361621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186361626 13194139535693 true] [17592186361621 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535693 true] [17592186361621 1041 "selection" 13194139535693 true] [17592186361621 1104 #edb/ref 17592186361625 13194139535693 true] [17592186361621 1110 #uuid "ee3233f5-30ea-5c0d-9fc0-bf2017770155" 13194139535693 true] [17592186361622 1034 "A paraboloid" 13194139535693 true] [17592186361622 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535693 true] [17592186361622 1111 #uuid "bfd184af-c443-5ce5-a494-704438845a83" 13194139535693 true] [17592186361623 1034 "A half-line" 13